PipeWire >= 1.4 only runs process() on a driving stream when the driver
triggers a graph cycle. The ext-image-copy backend already calls
pw_stream_trigger_process() after returning a frame; the zwlr_screencopy
backend does not, so the stream delivers one frame and then stalls.

Trigger from the screencopy frame callbacks, after wlr_frame_finish(),
so the (synchronous) process() callback captures a new frame only when
the stream is actually streaming. Triggering from inside
xdpw_pwr_enqueue_buffer() instead is wrong: the PAUSED state handler
enqueues before clearing pwr_stream_state, so process() dequeues a
buffer while pausing and the next STREAMING transition trips
assert(!cast->current_frame.pw_buffer).

--- a/src/screencast/wlr_screencopy.c
+++ b/src/screencast/wlr_screencopy.c
@@ -203,6 +203,7 @@ static void wlr_frame_ready(void *data, struct zwlr_screencopy_frame_v1 *frame,
 
 	xdpw_pwr_enqueue_buffer(cast);
 	wlr_frame_finish(cast);
+	pw_stream_trigger_process(cast->stream);
 }
 
 static void wlr_frame_failed(void *data,
@@ -215,6 +216,7 @@ static void wlr_frame_failed(void *data,
 
 	xdpw_pwr_enqueue_buffer(cast);
 	wlr_frame_finish(cast);
+	pw_stream_trigger_process(cast->stream);
 }
 
 static const struct zwlr_screencopy_frame_v1_listener wlr_frame_listener = {
